implementationPlan.description:
Adversarial self-review of your own diff, once, at the end; then complete_task with the step-3 evidence.

Size: git diff --numstat over the 3 files must total 400 changed lines or fewer (the prototype has 332). If it is over, file the epic size rail yourself.

Contract:
- '+' only after the job started.
- start EOF only after GUARD exits.
- GUARD exits only once init is reaped (namespace empty) and released, mirroring the job's exit or signal.
- No status is mirrored after a killed or failed init.
- Root whose unshare fails never starts the job.
- No threading anywhere in GUARD; the first line keeps the J4 prefix.

Races and edges:
- supervisor death before '+', between '+' and the status, after the status, and after stand-down;
- a group TERM during waitpid or select (handlers plus the PEP 475 retry);
- a job that SIGKILLs its own group (GUARD dies by SIGKILL; relay's EPIPE catch is unchanged);
- SIGCHLD SIG_IGN inherited: init's waitpid raises, no status is sent, and GUARD SIGKILLs (fail closed);
- report EOF and life EOF in one select batch;
- life EOF after init was reaped: never kill a reaped pid.

Security:
- no host-wide, name or UID kill, and no /proc scan-to-kill;
- no credential in any argv (J4's SECRET filter covers 4 ancestors);
- no env or CLI bypass;
- no verified-local or native-certification claim. CQ-R13 re-certifies against the new supervisorSha256.

Note, don't fix: the job still sees the guest's procfs (no mount namespace), so ps and pgrep inside it list outside pids. Native certification should exercise the real CLIs.

Mutation check: in a scratch copy, run the prototype memory's mutants against your bytes and record the kills:
- plain killpg on life EOF (G3);
- life unregistered after stand-down (G3);
- init waitpid(job) (G1);
- no exited check (G6).

CI wiring of the new file belongs to CQ-R13; the architect asked for it on f4811a72. Do not edit .github/workflows/ci.yml (dirty, peer-owned). This is a test-and-review step: no new runtime code.

invariants:
GUARD's private interface is unchanged:
- argv `life start spawnjson *argv`;
- exactly one '+' on start, written only once the job runs;
- start EOF only after GUARD has exited;
- GUARD's wait status equals the job's exit code or signal.

sourceAssumptions:
scripts/local-runner/guest-supervisor.py at HEAD c11348b4:
- header comment at lines 78-87;
- GUARD at lines 88-138;
- run_bridge (429-482) and run_install (1148-1176) start GUARD with start_new_session=True and pass_fds=(life, start).

scripts/tests/local-runner-bridge.test.mjs:
- J4's PROBE is around line 1295 and its assertions around 1334;
- S12 (around 1046) requires GUARD's imports to be stdlib and to include subprocess.

Linux PID namespaces:
- after unshare(CLONE_NEWPID), the next fork is pid 1 of the new namespace;
- that process's exit SIGKILLs and reaps every namespace member before it can itself be reaped;
- the unsharing process can no longer create threads;
- SIGKILL sent from the parent namespace reaches init.
All four were validated on this host.

planningNotes.approachesConsidered:
Chosen: a PID namespace plus a trusted init. It is the description's preferred mechanism, and it was validated on this host (sup-f prototype, Serena prototype memory). Rejected:
- a cgroup kill: it needs a delegated cgroupfs, and a cgroup sweep is ruled out;
- PR_SET_CHILD_SUBREAPER with a /proc scan: a scan-to-kill race, which the DoD forbids;
- a group or session kill: setsid and setpgid both escape it;
- keeping the watch thread: the kernel refuses CLONE_THREAD after unshare(CLONE_NEWPID);
- running NNP, netns and rlimits in init: a 109-line diff instead of 79, with no gain, because fork inherits them;
- a private /proc mount: it needs a mount namespace, so it is out of scope and left as a note for CQ-R13;
- separate root and non-root code paths: the non-root tests would stop exercising init.
